1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,929
Minus thirty degrees.

2
00:00:02,149 --> 00:00:02,874
Night.

3
00:00:03,094 --> 00:00:05,833
Somewhere in the mountains of North Korea,

4
00:00:05,833 --> 00:00:08,571
thirty thousand men are sleeping in frozen

5
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:09,174
foxholes.

6
00:00:09,644 --> 00:00:11,927
They think the war is almost over.

7
00:00:12,147 --> 00:00:13,868
They have been told they might be

8
00:00:13,868 --> 00:00:14,792
home by Christmas.

9
00:00:15,262 --> 00:00:16,755
Then the bugles start.

10
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:18,842
And out of the snow come more

11
00:00:18,842 --> 00:00:21,263
than a hundred thousand Chinese soldiers.

12
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:25,384
This is the Battle of Chosin Reservoir,

13
00:00:25,384 --> 00:00:26,704
November 1950.

14
00:00:26,924 --> 00:00:28,915
One of the most brutal fights in

15
00:00:28,915 --> 00:00:29,911
American history.

16
00:00:30,131 --> 00:00:32,507
If you like history told with real

17
00:00:32,507 --> 00:00:34,035
sources, tap like now.

18
00:00:34,255 --> 00:00:36,004
It really helps the channel.

19
00:00:38,674 --> 00:00:41,490
To understand Chosin, go back five months.

20
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:45,260
In June 1950, North Korea invaded the

21
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:45,891
South.

22
00:00:46,111 --> 00:00:48,339
The United Nations sent troops,

23
00:00:48,339 --> 00:00:49,964
most of them American,

24
00:00:49,964 --> 00:00:52,191
under General Douglas MacArthur.

25
00:00:52,661 --> 00:00:54,667
By the autumn, the war had turned.

26
00:00:54,887 --> 00:00:57,625
UN forces pushed deep into North Korea,

27
00:00:57,625 --> 00:01:00,364
racing toward the Chinese border on the

28
00:01:00,364 --> 00:01:01,180
Yalu River.

29
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:03,805
On the east side of the country,

30
00:01:03,805 --> 00:01:06,446
X Corps under General Edward Almond moved

31
00:01:06,446 --> 00:01:06,877
north.

32
00:01:07,097 --> 00:01:10,030
Its spearhead was the First Marine Division

33
00:01:10,030 --> 00:01:11,641
under General Oliver P.

34
00:01:11,861 --> 00:01:12,629
Smith.

35
00:01:12,849 --> 00:01:15,189
Their road led up into the mountains,

36
00:01:15,189 --> 00:01:17,373
to a man-made lake the maps called

37
00:01:17,373 --> 00:01:17,841
Chosin.

38
00:01:20,511 --> 00:01:22,047
Here is the problem.

39
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:24,488
Starting on the 19th of October,

40
00:01:24,488 --> 00:01:27,449
the Chinese People's Volunteer Army had been

41
00:01:27,449 --> 00:01:29,499
crossing into Korea in secret.

42
00:01:29,719 --> 00:01:31,616
They moved at night and hid by

43
00:01:31,616 --> 00:01:31,916
day.

44
00:01:32,386 --> 00:01:35,330
In the mountains around Chosin waited the

45
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:38,395
Chinese Ninth Army Group under General Song

46
00:01:38,395 --> 00:01:38,935
Shilun.

47
00:01:39,155 --> 00:01:40,350
How many men?

48
00:01:40,570 --> 00:01:43,047
The usual figure is about one hundred

49
00:01:43,047 --> 00:01:44,367
and twenty thousand.

50
00:01:44,837 --> 00:01:47,559
Britannica says twelve divisions and up to

51
00:01:47,559 --> 00:01:49,573
one hundred and fifty thousand.

52
00:01:49,793 --> 00:01:52,769
But prisoners later said their divisions were

53
00:01:52,769 --> 00:01:55,744
badly understrength, so some historians go as

54
00:01:55,744 --> 00:01:57,260
low as sixty thousand.

55
00:01:57,730 --> 00:02:00,012
Either way, the UN force of about

56
00:02:00,012 --> 00:02:02,573
thirty thousand was heavily outnumbered.

57
00:02:02,793 --> 00:02:04,265
And one more detail.

58
00:02:04,485 --> 00:02:06,953
Many of those Chinese soldiers had been

59
00:02:06,953 --> 00:02:09,946
rushed into Korea without proper winter clothing.

60
00:02:12,616 --> 00:02:14,748
Because the real enemy at Chosin was

61
00:02:14,748 --> 00:02:15,475
the weather.

62
00:02:15,695 --> 00:02:17,951
At night it fell to minus thirty

63
00:02:17,951 --> 00:02:20,772
Fahrenheit, about minus thirty-four Celsius.

64
00:02:20,992 --> 00:02:23,189
Some estimates go even lower.

65
00:02:23,659 --> 00:02:24,918
Rifles jammed.

66
00:02:25,138 --> 00:02:26,418
Batteries died.

67
00:02:26,638 --> 00:02:28,665
Food froze solid in the cans.

68
00:02:28,885 --> 00:02:30,634
Medical supplies froze.

69
00:02:30,854 --> 00:02:33,115
Wounded men could die of cold before

70
00:02:33,115 --> 00:02:34,502
they reached a doctor.

71
00:02:34,972 --> 00:02:37,282
More than seven thousand of the UN

72
00:02:37,282 --> 00:02:39,813
losses at Chosin were not from bullets

73
00:02:39,813 --> 00:02:41,628
at all, but from the cold.

74
00:02:44,298 --> 00:02:46,257
On the night of the 27th of

75
00:02:46,257 --> 00:02:48,159
November, the Chinese struck.

76
00:02:48,379 --> 00:02:51,061
They attacked the Marines at Yudam-ni,

77
00:02:51,061 --> 00:02:52,670
west of the reservoir,

78
00:02:52,670 --> 00:02:54,815
and at the same time hit the

79
00:02:54,815 --> 00:02:57,318
US Army regiment on the east side,

80
00:02:57,318 --> 00:03:00,060
and the bases at Hagaru-ri and Koto-ri

81
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:01,073
to the south.

82
00:03:01,543 --> 00:03:03,015
The plan was simple.

83
00:03:03,235 --> 00:03:05,472
Cut the one narrow road back to

84
00:03:05,472 --> 00:03:07,768
the sea, split the UN force into

85
00:03:07,768 --> 00:03:09,891
pieces, and destroy each piece.

86
00:03:10,361 --> 00:03:12,941
By morning, every UN position around the

87
00:03:12,941 --> 00:03:14,499
reservoir was surrounded.

88
00:03:14,969 --> 00:03:17,017
So here is my question for you.

89
00:03:17,237 --> 00:03:19,268
If you were in command that night,

90
00:03:19,268 --> 00:03:21,057
would you dig in and wait for

91
00:03:21,057 --> 00:03:22,894
help, or fight your way out at

92
00:03:22,894 --> 00:03:23,232
once?

93
00:03:23,452 --> 00:03:24,903
Tell me in the comments.

94
00:03:27,573 --> 00:03:29,237
The worst of it happened on the

95
00:03:29,237 --> 00:03:30,431
east side of the lake.

96
00:03:30,651 --> 00:03:33,951
About seventeen hundred American soldiers and some

97
00:03:33,951 --> 00:03:36,852
seven hundred and fifty South Korean troops

98
00:03:36,852 --> 00:03:38,161
were dug in there,

99
00:03:38,161 --> 00:03:40,721
a force later named Task Force Faith.

100
00:03:41,191 --> 00:03:43,473
Their commander, Colonel Allan Maclean,

101
00:03:43,473 --> 00:03:45,704
went forward to meet what he thought

102
00:03:45,704 --> 00:03:46,972
were friendly troops.

103
00:03:47,192 --> 00:03:48,515
They were Chinese.

104
00:03:48,735 --> 00:03:49,844
He was lost.

105
00:03:50,064 --> 00:03:52,624
Lieutenant Colonel Don Faith took command.

106
00:03:53,094 --> 00:03:54,656
On the first of December,

107
00:03:54,656 --> 00:03:56,872
Faith led a breakout south along the

108
00:03:56,872 --> 00:03:57,275
shore.

109
00:03:57,495 --> 00:03:59,526
American planes, trying to help,

110
00:03:59,526 --> 00:04:02,251
accidentally dropped napalm on the front of

111
00:04:02,251 --> 00:04:02,999
the column.

112
00:04:03,219 --> 00:04:05,267
Faith was killed leading the attack.

113
00:04:05,487 --> 00:04:07,363
He was given the Medal of Honor

114
00:04:07,363 --> 00:04:08,325
after his death.

115
00:04:08,795 --> 00:04:10,309
How many made it out?

116
00:04:10,529 --> 00:04:12,108
The sources disagree.

117
00:04:12,328 --> 00:04:14,820
One count says only about three hundred

118
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:17,259
and eighty-five men were still able to

119
00:04:17,259 --> 00:04:17,683
fight.

120
00:04:17,903 --> 00:04:20,810
Britannica says around six hundred and seventy

121
00:04:20,810 --> 00:04:23,179
reached safety, half of them fit for

122
00:04:23,179 --> 00:04:23,556
duty.

123
00:04:23,776 --> 00:04:26,005
Either way, most of the task force

124
00:04:26,005 --> 00:04:28,021
was killed, wounded or captured.

125
00:04:30,691 --> 00:04:31,778
West of the lake,

126
00:04:31,778 --> 00:04:33,804
the Marines were in danger of the

127
00:04:33,804 --> 00:04:34,446
same fate.

128
00:04:34,666 --> 00:04:36,403
On the 30th of November,

129
00:04:36,403 --> 00:04:39,125
the order came: pull back to Hagaru-ri.

130
00:04:39,595 --> 00:04:41,766
General Smith refused to call it a

131
00:04:41,766 --> 00:04:42,283
retreat.

132
00:04:42,503 --> 00:04:43,821
As the story goes,

133
00:04:43,821 --> 00:04:45,425
he said: Retreat, hell.

134
00:04:45,645 --> 00:04:47,891
We are just advancing in a different

135
00:04:47,891 --> 00:04:48,504
direction.

136
00:04:48,724 --> 00:04:51,053
Historians agree on the second half of

137
00:04:51,053 --> 00:04:51,711
that line.

138
00:04:51,931 --> 00:04:53,776
The first half may have been added

139
00:04:53,776 --> 00:04:54,128
later.

140
00:04:54,598 --> 00:04:56,881
Either way, the Marines did not run.

141
00:04:57,101 --> 00:04:59,125
They fought for every hill along the

142
00:04:59,125 --> 00:04:59,447
road.

143
00:04:59,667 --> 00:05:02,033
On the 2nd of December they opened

144
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:04,737
Toktong Pass, where one company had held

145
00:05:04,737 --> 00:05:06,089
out alone for days.

146
00:05:06,309 --> 00:05:08,637
By the 4th, the Yudam-ni force had

147
00:05:08,637 --> 00:05:11,962
reached Hagaru-ri, bringing their wounded with them.

148
00:05:14,632 --> 00:05:17,391
From Hagaru-ri, on the 6th of December,

149
00:05:17,391 --> 00:05:19,974
the whole column started south again,

150
00:05:19,974 --> 00:05:22,909
fighting through roadblocks the entire way.

151
00:05:23,379 --> 00:05:25,301
Then came the moment that could have

152
00:05:25,301 --> 00:05:26,174
ended everything.

153
00:05:26,394 --> 00:05:29,031
At Funchilin Pass, the Chinese blew up

154
00:05:29,031 --> 00:05:30,981
a bridge over a deep chasm.

155
00:05:31,201 --> 00:05:32,836
With no bridge, the trucks,

156
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:34,916
the guns and the wounded could not

157
00:05:34,916 --> 00:05:35,659
get through.

158
00:05:36,129 --> 00:05:37,857
The answer came from the air.

159
00:05:38,077 --> 00:05:40,571
A new bridge was dropped by parachute,

160
00:05:40,571 --> 00:05:41,384
in sections.

161
00:05:41,604 --> 00:05:43,834
Enough of them survived the fall for

162
00:05:43,834 --> 00:05:45,609
engineers to put it together,

163
00:05:45,609 --> 00:05:47,129
and the vehicles crossed.

164
00:05:47,599 --> 00:05:49,099
By the 11th of December,

165
00:05:49,099 --> 00:05:51,549
the First Marine Division had reached the

166
00:05:51,549 --> 00:05:52,549
port of Hungnam.

167
00:05:55,219 --> 00:05:57,336
What came next was one of the

168
00:05:57,336 --> 00:05:59,912
largest evacuations by sea in history.

169
00:06:00,132 --> 00:06:01,521
In about two weeks,

170
00:06:01,521 --> 00:06:04,299
nearly two hundred ships carried out one

171
00:06:04,299 --> 00:06:06,673
hundred and five thousand soldiers,

172
00:06:06,673 --> 00:06:09,104
ninety-one thousand Korean civilians,

173
00:06:09,104 --> 00:06:12,461
seventeen thousand five hundred vehicles and three

174
00:06:12,461 --> 00:06:15,471
hundred and fifty thousand tons of supplies.

175
00:06:15,941 --> 00:06:17,761
On Christmas Eve 1950,

176
00:06:17,761 --> 00:06:19,514
the last troops left,

177
00:06:19,514 --> 00:06:22,210
and the port was blown up behind

178
00:06:22,210 --> 00:06:22,682
them.

179
00:06:25,352 --> 00:06:27,483
The US estimate of UN losses:

180
00:06:27,483 --> 00:06:30,087
about one thousand and thirty killed,

181
00:06:30,087 --> 00:06:32,514
four thousand five hundred wounded,

182
00:06:32,514 --> 00:06:35,415
nearly four thousand nine hundred missing,

183
00:06:35,415 --> 00:06:38,375
and more than seven thousand cold injuries

184
00:06:38,375 --> 00:06:40,328
and other non-battle losses.

185
00:06:40,798 --> 00:06:43,273
Chinese losses are even less certain.

186
00:06:43,493 --> 00:06:46,573
Chinese figures list about seven thousand three

187
00:06:46,573 --> 00:06:49,933
hundred killed, fourteen thousand wounded and thirty

188
00:06:49,933 --> 00:06:53,125
thousand non-battle casualties, most of them from

189
00:06:53,125 --> 00:06:53,797
the cold.

190
00:06:54,017 --> 00:06:56,877
Britannica puts the total between forty and

191
00:06:56,877 --> 00:06:57,942
eighty thousand.

192
00:06:58,412 --> 00:06:59,564
So who won?

193
00:06:59,784 --> 00:07:01,469
On the map, China did.

194
00:07:01,689 --> 00:07:03,588
The UN left North Korea.

195
00:07:03,808 --> 00:07:06,838
Britannica calls it a geographic victory for

196
00:07:06,838 --> 00:07:07,712
the Chinese.

197
00:07:07,932 --> 00:07:10,852
But the UN force was not destroyed,

198
00:07:10,852 --> 00:07:13,840
and the Chinese Ninth Army Group was

199
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:16,691
so badly damaged that it could not

200
00:07:16,691 --> 00:07:19,068
fight again until March 1951.

201
00:07:21,738 --> 00:07:25,137
In November 1950, UN troops stood near

202
00:07:25,137 --> 00:07:26,837
the Chinese border.

203
00:07:27,057 --> 00:07:28,910
A month later they were back below

204
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:30,278
the thirty-eighth parallel.

205
00:07:30,498 --> 00:07:32,579
The war went on until 1953,

206
00:07:32,579 --> 00:07:35,027
and the line where it stopped is

207
00:07:35,027 --> 00:07:37,964
still the border between North and South

208
00:07:37,964 --> 00:07:38,882
Korea today.

209
00:07:39,352 --> 00:07:42,237
The Chosin Reservoir is now inside North

210
00:07:42,237 --> 00:07:44,941
Korea, closed to almost every visitor.

211
00:07:45,161 --> 00:07:47,393
The men who fought there call themselves

212
00:07:47,393 --> 00:07:48,276
the Chosin Few.

213
00:07:48,746 --> 00:07:50,401
So, a defeat, a victory,

214
00:07:50,401 --> 00:07:51,007
or both?

215
00:07:51,227 --> 00:07:52,822
Tell me what you think in the

216
00:07:52,822 --> 00:07:53,297
comments.

217
00:07:53,517 --> 00:07:56,121
And subscribe, because next time we go

218
00:07:56,121 --> 00:07:57,820
back eight hundred years,

219
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:00,085
to the city of Samarkand and the

220
00:08:00,085 --> 00:08:02,519
day Genghis Khan came to its gates.

